What each item became

1. Registry through raster_io + composite — every PIL.Image.open in the inference path is gone. Pair tasks call align_pair, which reprojects onto a common grid rather than assuming identical bounds. Composites and prompt strings now come from the same modules/composite.py that the training scripts import, so train/serve skew is structurally impossible.

2. run_optical_sar crash — signature kept at four arguments (it was always right); the controller's three-argument call was the bug. Fixed. The path now actually executes, and does real work: modules/sar_analysis.py derives water from specular radar-dark returns and built-up from bright high-texture double-bounce, cross-checked against optical brightness to reject radar shadow.

3. Fabricated trace — tool_descriptors() returns real model identifiers. Every tool returns applied_parameters containing what it actually consumed, not what was requested. Each descriptor carries remote_sensing_adapted, which is false for GroundingDINO — that gap is now visible in the UI instead of hidden behind "GroundingDINO-RS (Active)".

4. geo_validator — tolerance is one pixel, computed after transforming both footprints into a common CRS. Five statuses; only two say "verified". PNG pairs report "alignment not verifiable" and proceed. The boolean shim now returns False for unverifiable pairs rather than True.

5. Change detection — interquartile-anchored radiometric normalisation → change vector analysis → Otsu → morphology → connected components, overlaid on T2 rather than floating on black. Otsu separability is reported as evidence quality, since Otsu always returns some split.

6. Offline router + eval harness — modules/router.py classifies all five representative queries deterministically with no network. The controller tries Gemini, falls back silently, and records which backend decided and why. eval/run_benchmarks.py scores exact match, token F1, grounding IoU, and — importantly — distinct-prediction ratio and majority-class baseline, so a collapsed adapter can't hide behind a decent-looking accuracy number.

One thing I changed beyond the list

Writing the tests exposed a real weakness in my own change-detection code. I had anchored radiometric normalisation on p2/p98, reasoning that changed pixels are a minority. They are — but a change region covering 10% of a scene at extreme brightness sits inside the 98th percentile, so the anchor gets computed from the change itself and drags the unchanged background off by tens of DN. Switched to p25/p75, which tolerates 25% contamination in either tail. That's fixed in the code, not papered over in the test.

